.TH CPPAUTOMATA 1 "October 2026" "cppautomata 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cppautomata \- Conway's Game of Life as a terminal screensaver
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cppautomata
.RB [ \-c
.IR color ]
.RB [ \-b
.IR color ]
.RB [ \-v
.IR ms ]
.RB [ \-s
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR hh:mm:ss ]
.RB [ \-m
.IR mode ]
.RB [ \-r ]
.RB [ \-h ]
.RI [ glyph ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cppautomata
runs Conway's Game of Life full screen in the terminal using ncurses.
The grid takes the size of the terminal at startup, and its opposite edges
are identified, so the board is a torus: a pattern leaving on the right
comes back on the left, and the same holds for top and bottom.
.PP
At every generation each cell looks at its eight neighbours and the
standard rules apply:
.IP \(bu 2
a live cell with fewer than 2 live neighbours dies;
.IP \(bu 2
a live cell with 2 or 3 live neighbours survives;
.IP \(bu 2
a live cell with more than 3 live neighbours dies;
.IP \(bu 2
a dead cell with exactly 3 live neighbours becomes alive.
.PP
.I glyph
is the string drawn for a live cell. The default is a full block
(\[u2588]). Any UTF\-8 string works, for example an emoji or a Nerd Font
icon, provided the terminal font can render it. Dead cells are drawn as
a blank space.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \--version
See cppautomata version
.TP
.BI \-c " color"
Foreground color of live cells. Default:
.BR red .
See
.B COLORS
for the accepted names.
.TP
.BI \-b " color"
Background color. Default:
.BR black .
.TP
.BI \-v " ms"
Milliseconds between two generations, integer greater than 0.
Default:
.BR 100 .
.TP
.BI \-s " n"
Restart from the initial pattern every
.I n
generations. 0 means never. Default:
.BR 0 .
.TP
.BI \-t " hh:mm:ss"
Restart from the initial pattern after the given amount of run time,
written as hours, minutes and seconds separated by colons, for example
.BR 00:05:00 .
.TP
.BI \-m " mode"
Initial pattern. One of:
.RS
.TP
.B random
every cell is alive with probability 1/2 (default);
.TP
.B glider
a single glider in the top left corner;
.TP
.B blinker
a single period 2 blinker.
.RE
.TP
.B \-r
Rainbow mode: change the foreground color at every generation, cycling
through the 256 color palette.
.TP
.B \-h
Print a short help and exit.
.SH COLORS
.BR black ,
.BR red ,
.BR green ,
.BR yellow ,
.BR blue ,
.BR magenta ,
.BR cyan ,
.BR white .
.SH INTERACTIVE COMMANDS
.TP
.B q
Quit.
.TP
.B r
Restart from the initial pattern.
.TP
.B c
Switch to the next foreground color.
.TP
.B b
Switch to the next background color.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B cppautomata
Random start, red full blocks on black, 100 ms per generation.
.TP
.B cppautomata \-c green \-v 50 \-s 500
Green cells, 50 ms per generation, restart every 500 generations.
.TP
.B cppautomata \-m glider \-c cyan o
A single glider drawn with the letter
.BR o .
.TP
.B cppautomata \-r \-t 00:10:00 \[u25CF]
Rainbow mode with round dots, restarting every 10 minutes.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Normal exit, or after
.BR \-h .
.TP
.B 1
Unknown option or missing option argument.
.SH NOTES
The grid size is read once at startup: resizing the terminal while the
program runs does not resize the board.
.PP
The
.B \-t
timer counts the sleep intervals set by
.BR \-v ,
not wall clock time, so with a large grid the real time before a restart
can be longer than requested.
.SH AUTHOR
Riccardo Martelli
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cmatrix (1),
.BR asciiquarium (1),
.BR ncurses (3X)
